{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 6, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.90,
      "why": "Only unrolled_k can plausibly meet the tight batch-latency constraint. With k=8 and N=12, cycles/result = ceil(12/8)+3 = 5, so 32 results take 160 cycles; at 0.44 us this needs Fmax >= 363 MHz. k=6..8 and N=10..16 explore the trade-off between area (lower k, lower N, lower W) and accuracy (higher N, higher W, guards). The ranges cover the minimal area that can still achieve max_abs_err <= 2^-12."
    },
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.10,
      "why": "Iterative is very unlikely to meet the batch-latency constraint: cycles/result = N+3, so 32 results take 32*(N+3) cycles. For N>=12 (needed for 2^-12 accuracy), this requires Fmax >= 480 MHz, which is implausible on Artix-7. A small budget confirms infeasibility and checks whether any low-N, low-W corner could unexpectedly pass the L1 bound."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The system scenario demands 32 results within 0.44 us of a 1 MHz tick (p99 batch latency). This is a hard batch-latency constraint that dominates family choice. Iterative processes one result every N+3 cycles, so for N>=12 it would need >480 MHz to meet the batch deadline. Unrolled_k with high k (6..8) reduces cycles/result to ceil(N/k)+3; with k=8 and N=12 this is 5 cycles, requiring ~363 MHz, which is at least plausible. Therefore 90% of the budget goes to unrolled_k over wide ranges of data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding, and k to map the area-accuracy trade-off and find the smallest design that satisfies max_abs_err <= 2^-12 and the batch bound. The remaining 10% probes iterative to confirm it cannot meet the constraints, avoiding wasted budget on a likely infeasible family."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "budget_share": 0.75,
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 14, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
        ]
      ,
        "why": "1 result/cycle = Fmax MSPS is the only geometry that can drain a 32-request burst; latency N+2 keeps sys_p99_batch_us small at the higher Fmax a single-micro-rotation stage allows."
      },
      {
        "budget_share": 0.25,
        "family": "pipelined_m",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 14, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
        ],
        "why": "Same 1 result/cycle, fewer registers -> lower luts_plus_ffs objective; explore alongside pipelined to fill the area end of the front."
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Round 1 explored only the two sub-unity-throughput families (iterative, unrolled_k), so 100% of the 60 evals miss throughput_msps>=32 (best 13.5) and 100% miss sys_p99_batch_us<=0.44 (best 2.36): these families accept ~1 request per N/k cycles and therefore queue a 32-request burst, which the burst-sensitive sys_p99_batch_us penalises directly. The accuracy axis is already reachable (best max_abs_err 9.31e-05 = 2^-13.39, and designs at n_iter=15 clear 2^-12), so the binding limits are purely throughput/burst. pipelined (1 result/cycle, N+2 latency) and pipelined_m (1 result/cycle, ceil(N/m)+2 latency) are the only registry families that can supply 32 results within 0.44 us while keeping luts_plus_ffs competitive, so spend the remaining budget bringing them onto the front. Keep rounding=round and W 14..20 / N 12..20 since that region already shows the 2^-12 accuracy is achievable with margin."
  }
  ,
  "rationale": "Only sequential-throughput families (iterative, unrolled_k) have been sampled, giving 0 feasible designs and a hypervolume of 0: throughput 100% violated (best 13.5 MSPS) and sys_p99_batch_us 100% violated (best 2.36 vs 0.44). Both failures are structural, not parametric - one result every several cycles cannot drain 32 simultaneous requests inside the tick deadline, no matter how W/N/k are tuned. Accuracy is not the blocker (best seen 2^-13.39, already past the 2^-12 limit). The unexplored pipelined and pipelined_m families produce 1 result/cycle and are the only way to meet throughput_msps>=32 and the 0.44 us batch bound, so add them now rather than refining the sequential ranges."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Budget is exhausted (100/100 evals, final round), so the only admissible choices are stop or infeasible — and infeasible is not supported by the evidence. The two constraints that never passed are throughput_msps >= 32 and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44, but both were only ever measured on the registry's MULTI-CYCLE families: iterative (N+3 cycles/result, max 23.7 MSPS seen) and unrolled_k (ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result, max 14.8 MSPS; best batch 1.35 us = 3.1x over budget). Those families are structurally incapable of the >32 MSPS needed to drain 32 requests in 0.44 us, which is exactly why 0 of 100 designs are feasible and hypervolume is 0. The registry also contains pipelined (1 result/cycle) and pipelined_m, which were never sampled by any round; a 1-result/cycle design clears 32 MSPS at any Fmax above ~32 MHz and 32 back-to-back requests in ~32 cycles, so the evidence does NOT show the constraints are unreachable. The accuracy target is clearly reachable (best max_abs_err 2.86e-07 = 2^-21.7, and 2^-12.63 at data_width=17/n_iter=15, both inside 2^-12), so accuracy is not the blocker. Verdict: no feasible design was found within the evaluation budget, and the search covered only the half of the registry that cannot satisfy the burst requirement; this is a budget-ended 'no feasible design found', not a proven infeasible registry. No design can be selected, and the front has no non-violating member to rank, so the run stops here."
}
